  "common_pitfalls": [
    "Using LLMs to verify LLM-generated content creates circular dependencies that reviewers immediately flag — GSM-Symbolic's manual templates, FoVer's Z3/Isabelle verification, and PAV's probability-based progress scoring all succeed precisely because they avoid this. CRADE must use SymPy/Z3 for variant verification, not LLM-as-judge.",
    "Treating contamination as binary (present/absent) when it's continuous and regime-dependent — How Much Can We Forget shows contamination follows dose-response curves with forgetting dynamics. ConStat's R²=0.94 quantification and the proposed c_ij per-instance indicators are essential; binary detection is a saturated space.",
    "Evaluating only on final-answer accuracy when the contribution is about process-level reasoning — ReasonEval showed answer-level overestimates capability by ~1.4 points, ProcessBench showed PRMs collapse to 23.8 F1 on hard problems, and Chen et al. showed 75% CoT unfaithfulness. Any CRADE paper that reports only answer accuracy will be rejected as not practicing what it preaches.",
