  ],

  "field_trajectory": "The field is converging on three macro-directions that will define 2026-2027 research. First, measurement-theoretic foundations for LLM evaluation are rapidly gaining traction: PSN-IRT (AAAI 2026 Oral), tinyBenchmarks (ICML 2024), and the Measuring What Matters construct validity framework (NeurIPS 2025) collectively establish that raw accuracy aggregation is scientifically indefensible, pushing the community toward psychometrics-grounded evaluation. This creates demand for contamination-aware psychometric models — the natural next step that no one has built yet. Second, process-level evaluation is becoming non-negotiable: ReasonEval (AAAI 2025 Oral), ProcessBench (ACL 2025), PAV (ICLR 2025 Spotlight), and ThinkPRM demonstrate that step-level analysis captures capabilities invisible to answer-level metrics, while Chen et al. and FaithCoT-Bench establish that even the reasoning process itself may be unreliable. The tension between 'we must evaluate reasoning processes' and 'reasoning processes may be unfaithful' is the field's central paradox, and resolving it — likely through formal/behavioral signals rather than CoT inspection — will define the next generation of evaluation methods. Third, contamination science is maturing from detection into quantification and correction: ConStat redefined contamination as non-generalization, How Much Can We Forget established forgetting dynamics, Self-Critique addressed the RL blind spot, and MathArena provides temporal ground truth. The field is ready for a unified framework that consumes contamination signals and produces corrected ability estimates — moving from 'is this model contaminated?' to 'what is this model's genuine capability?' The convergence point of these three trajectories — psychometric scoring that is contamination-aware and process-grounded — is exactly where CRADE sits, suggesting the timing is optimal for a framework paper at this intersection.",

  "meta_advice": "If I were advising a PhD student targeting a strong 2026 paper in contamination-aware LLM evaluation, I would give five specific directives. First, position at the three-way intersection — contamination detection, adversarial robustness, and psychometric evaluation are three separate communities with different venues and different authors (detection at EMNLP/NeurIPS, robustness at ICLR, psychometrics at AAAI/ICML). Papers that bridge two of these (ConStat bridges detection+evaluation; MaSTer bridges adversarial+evaluation) already succeed; bridging all three is the white space. Second, ground everything in IRT measurement theory — PSN-IRT got an AAAI 2026 Oral, tinyBenchmarks got ICML 2024, and the construct validity survey got NeurIPS D&B. The psychometric angle is what elevates 'contamination-adjusted scores' from an engineering trick to a scientific contribution; without it, you're building a pipeline, not a framework. Third, use formal verification (SymPy, Z3) instead of LLM-based generation or verification everywhere possible — FoVer demonstrated cross-domain transfer from formally verified to natural language, and reviewers in 2025-2026 are actively penalizing circular LLM-evaluates-LLM designs. The 'no LLMs in the variant generation loop' constraint is not just philosophical purity; it's what makes your correctness claims credible. Fourth, validate against MathArena's uncontaminated competition data as your external ground truth — this is the single most convincing validation because it's independent of your own contamination estimates and is already accepted by the community as a contamination-free reference. If your calibrated θ scores predict MathArena performance better than raw accuracy does, that's your headline result. Fifth, don't neglect the RL-trained model regime — Kocetkov et al. (Oct 2025) established that existing methods fail, Self-Critique (ICLR 2026) showed entropy collapse works, and Yue et al. (NeurIPS 2025 Best Paper Runner-Up) proved contamination persists through RL. Any framework paper that ignores o1/R1-class models will be seen as solving yesterday's problem. The combination of these five elements — three-way intersection positioning, IRT grounding, formal verification, MathArena validation, and RL-model coverage — is what separates a solid workshop paper from a main-conference oral."
